{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# A per-pass model configuration seam (one env var cannot express four passes)\n\n## Problem\n\nrebar's gates deliberately run different model classes per pass. On the direct-Anthropic path this\nworks today and is MEASURED: a real `rebar review-plan` made 41 calls on `anthropic:claude-opus-4-8`\n(Pass-1, which escalates up from the YAML `model_ladder`) and 1 on `anthropic:claude-sonnet-4-6`\n(the Pass-2/Pass-4 verifier downgrade). The operator requirement is exactly that shape: Pass-1 on\nopus for most criteria, Pass-2/Pass-4 on sonnet.\n\nThere is no way to express that on any non-default provider, because the whole selection collapses\nonto ONE knob:\n\n- ~~**The verifier downgrade inverts.**~~ **RESOLVED — retained only so the record shows what changed.**\n  This bullet argued that `review_kernel/verify.py:269`'s `verifier_default if model == default_model\n  else (model or verifier_default)` would read ANY Bedrock id as an explicit operator choice, so the\n  sonnet downgrade would stop applying and Pass-2/Pass-4 would follow Pass-1 onto one model. That\n  mechanism is now UNREACHABLE: `resolve_verifier_model` has ZERO callers under `src/rebar`, pinned by\n  `test_the_superseded_equality_rule_has_no_production_consumers` (172e). The verifier paths resolve the\n  `standard` CLASS instead, so the inversion cannot occur. What remains is narrower and is the real\n  subject of this ticket: four code-review steps declare no class at all.\n- ~~**Pass-1's ladder routes to the wrong provider.**~~ **RESOLVED — this problem no longer exists, and\n  the claim is retained only so the record is honest about what changed.** This section originally read\n  that `plan-review.yaml:62-65` sets `model_ladder: [claude-haiku-4-5, claude-sonnet-4-6,\n  claude-opus-4-8]` as BARE names, and that since `config.py` maps the `claude` prefix to provider\n  `anthropic`, a Bedrock run would send its Pass-1 finders to DIRECT ANTHROPIC — a split brain. VERIFIED\n  AT HEAD: that ladder now reads `[trivial, standard, frontier]`, i.e. CLASS terms (story 7761 landed\n  it), so the bare-name provider inference no longer applies to plan-review's Pass-1. The split-brain\n  risk this ticket was partly created to fix is already closed for plan-review; what REMAINS is the\n  code-review gate, below.\n- **Code review is only PARTLY split — this is the remaining problem and the reason this ticket is still\n  needed.** CORRECTED against HEAD: an earlier version said `code-review.yaml` \"declares no `model:` and\n  no `model_ladder:` on any step\", which is no longer true — `verify` (`:143`) declares `model: standard`\n  at `:156` (story 172e landed it). The four steps that still inherit `cfg.model` implicitly are `base`\n  (`:67`), `round_a` (`:77`), `round_b` (`:109`) and `coach` (`:182`). So the gate has a Pass-2 split and\n  no Pass-1/Pass-4 split. Note the four undeclared steps do NOT fall back to `resolve_verifier_model` either — nothing under\n  `src/rebar` calls it. They simply inherit the run's single resolved `cfg.model`, having declared no\n  per-step model for the interpreter to override with.\n\n## Why this blocks two stories\n\n- **eb6e (production cutover)** scopes itself to \"the bot service's `REBAR_LLM_MODEL`\" — one env var\n  for four passes. Both single-model outcomes are wrong: opus-everywhere hard-fails Pass-2, which\n  pins `temperature=0` (MEASURED: `us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8` returns 400 `temperature is\n  deprecated for this model`; see [rebar:1903-868c-b704-4a80]), and sonnet-everywhere silently\n  downgrades Pass-1 with no test to catch it.\n- **0d76 (parity bar)** compares v1=Anthropic against v2=Bedrock. Without per-pass control the arms\n  differ in TWO variables (provider AND per-pass model class), so any criterion failure is\n  unattributable and any pass is unrepresentative of production. A full corpus run is ~3,200 model\n  calls; spending that on an unattributable comparison is the expensive way to learn this.\n\n## Approach (the mechanism EXISTS — this ticket is the CONSUMER)\n\n**THE EXACT CLASS EACH STEP DECLARES — stated here so no executor has to guess.** A review round\nblocked twice on this being unspecified.\n\n| step | `code-review.yaml` | pass | declares |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `base`    | `:67`  | Pass-1 finder (single-shot) | `model: frontier` |\n| `round_a` | `:77`  | Pass-1 finder (**batch**)   | inside `batch:` -> `model_ladder: [frontier]` |\n| `round_b` | `:109` | Pass-1 finder (**batch**)   | inside `batch:` -> `model_ladder: [frontier]` |\n| `verify`  | `:143` | Pass-2 verifier             | `model: standard` — ALREADY LANDED (172e) |\n| `coach`   | `:182` | Pass-4 coach                | step-level `model: standard` |\n\nThis matches the criterion above — opus-family for Pass-1, sonnet-family for Pass-2/Pass-4 — because\n`frontier` and `standard` resolve to exactly those families via `_DEFAULT_MODEL_BY_CLASS`.\n\n**WHY THE BATCH STEPS MUST USE A LADDER KEY, AND WHY IT HAS EXACTLY ONE RUNG.** A step-level `model:` on\na `batch:` step is a SILENT NO-OP — `interpreter.py:384-395` builds `BatchRunRequest` from the `batch:`\nsub-dict only (`model_ladder=tuple(batch.get('model_ladder') or ())`) and never passes the enclosing\nstep's `model:`, while `runners.py:128-130` computes `resolve_model_string(ladder[0]) if ladder else\nNone` and comments that an empty ladder means \"no per-step model, NOT a resolved class\". Because `model`\nIS a legal step key (`schema.py` `_STEP_ORDER`), such a declaration VALIDATES and is then discarded —\nand a test asserting only that the YAML carries a class token would PASS while runtime behaviour was\nunchanged. An earlier version of this very table made that mistake.\nONE RUNG, not plan-review's three:\n`plan-review.yaml:62-65` declares its finder batch as `model_ladder: [trivial, standard, frontier]`, so\ncopying that precedent here is the obvious move — and it is the WRONG one for this ticket. A ladder\nescalates per criterion, so the model that actually answers a given criterion is NOT determinate: a\ncheap criterion may be resolved at `trivial` and never reach `frontier`. This ticket's first criterion\nrequires a TEST asserting \"the RESOLVED model for each pass ... is the intended class\", and that\nassertion is not writable against a non-deterministic resolution. Flat classes keep the criterion\nfalsifiable. Adopting the ladder for cost reasons is a legitimate FOLLOW-UP optimisation, but it is a\ndifferent change with a different oracle, and bundling it here would make the per-pass guarantee\nuntestable — which is the whole point of the ticket.\n\n**PASSES vs STEPS — two different counts, both correct, stated so they are not read as a\ncontradiction.** \"Four passes\" is the plan-review PASS model (Pass-1 finder, Pass-2 verifier, Pass-3\ndecide, Pass-4 coach) and is what this ticket's title means. \"Five LLM-calling steps\" is the CODE-REVIEW\ngate's step count in `code-review.yaml`. One pass can be implemented by MORE THAN ONE step — which is\nprecisely how the batch steps `round_a`/`round_b` escaped an earlier count. Neither number is wrong;\nthey measure different things.\n\n**REWRITTEN: an earlier draft called this a \"candidate, not a decision\" and sketched a mapping still to\nbe designed. Stale — epic eb58 has since shipped it.** `model_classes.py` provides the\ntrivial/standard/frontier slots, the nine `REBAR_LLM_<CLASS>_<FIELD>` overrides, per-class fallback\nchains and class-aware resolution; the batch runners and verifier paths already consume it; and\n`REBAR_LLM_MODEL` is deprecated, fanning out to all three classes. This ticket does NOT design a\nmapping — it makes the remaining gate steps DECLARE their class.\n\nMake the per-pass model resolvable per provider rather than inferred from one string. Sketch:\nexpress the mapping (Pass-1 finder ladder, Pass-2/Pass-4 verifier) as configuration keyed by\nprovider, so a Bedrock run names Bedrock inference-profile ids for each pass instead of inheriting\nbare `claude-*` names. Note `bedrock_model_id` is NOT the answer — a single scalar cannot express a\nmapping, which is part of why it was deleted [rebar:281f-3336-c668-4979].\n\n**STALENESS AUDIT of this section, run against source at HEAD 18ecd9709 rather than accepted wholesale\nfrom a review finding:**\n* STALE, fixed: `plan-review.yaml` no longer names bare `claude-*` ids — `:62` declares a `model_ladder`\n  in CLASS terms (7761).\n* STALE, fixed: \"no per-provider class mapping exists\" — `model_classes.py` exists and is consumed.\n* **RETRACTED — I DEFENDED THIS CLAIM AND IT WAS WRONG.** An earlier revision asserted that\n  `review_kernel/verify.py:263-269`'s equality test (`verifier_default if model == default_model else\n  ...`) is OPERATIVE, and that steps without a declared class \"still inherit the equality rule\". The\n  function body does still contain that expression — which is all I actually checked — but it has\n  **ZERO production call sites**: `resolve_verifier_model` appears under `src/rebar` only as its own\n  definition, one docstring mention in `model_classes.py:279`, and two re-export lines in\n  `review_kernel/__init__.py`. `tests/unit/test_code_review_verifier_class.py:171`\n  (`test_the_superseded_equality_rule_has_no_production_consumers`, landed by 172e) ENFORCES that\n  nothing calls it, and instructs \"do not relax the assertion\". So the rule is superseded and\n  unreachable, not live. I verified EXISTENCE and inferred REACHABILITY, which is not the same thing.\n* **THE REAL MECHANISM, corrected:** the four undeclared steps inherit the run's single resolved\n  `cfg.model` because they declare no per-step model at all — the interpreter simply has nothing to\n  override with. That single-knob inheritance is what this ticket removes. `resolve_verifier_model` is\n  NOT part of the story and must not be cited as the defect; if anything, a NEW caller of it would\n  reintroduce the 172e defect and break the guard test.\n\nThis ticket does NOT touch `verify.py:269`. An earlier revision said the chosen shape \"must also fix\"\nthat equality test; that instruction rested on the retracted claim above. The helper is superseded and\ncallerless, a guard test pins it that way, and ADDING a caller would reintroduce the defect 172e\nremoved. Leave it alone.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] a test asserts the RESOLVED model for each pass under a Bedrock configuration is the intended\n      class (opus-family for Pass-1, sonnet-family for Pass-2/Pass-4)\n- [ ] a test asserts no bare `claude-*` model id is resolved to provider `anthropic` during a run\n      configured for another provider\n- [ ] a test asserts the Pass-2/Pass-4 downgrade still applies when the operator has NOT explicitly\n      chosen a model, on a non-default provider\n- [ ] the code-review gate expresses its per-pass models explicitly. RE-MEASURED against the shipped YAML — and this CORRECTS AN ERROR AN EARLIER PASS OF THIS PLAN\n      INTRODUCED. That pass wrote \"the gate has THREE prompt steps, not four\", which is WRONG: it\n      counted only the single-shot prompt steps and MISSED THE TWO BATCH STEPS. The gate has FIVE\n      LLM-calling steps — `base` (:67), `round_a` (:77), `round_b` (:109), `verify` (:143) and `coach`\n      (:182). Only `verify` declares `model: standard` (:156, landed by 172e). The remaining work is\n      therefore FOUR steps — `base`, `round_a`, `round_b`, `coach` — all still inheriting `cfg.model`.\n      Correcting \"four\" DOWN to \"three\" made this plan worse than its original wording, and would\n      have shipped two batch steps silently unclassed\n- [ ] [operator-attested] a real gate run on the non-default provider shows the per-pass model ids\n      actually used, cited from the run's usage log in a comment"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785601079576516001,"uuid":"f04770a3-5911-4459-af54-45f7725aa762"}